WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
Did Robin make an appearance in "The&nbsp;
Batman" without anybody noticing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.800
Well, yes…and no. Keep watching to&nbsp;
find out how the Boy Wonder appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.680
alongside Robert Pattinson's&nbsp;
Dark Knight in "The Batman"!

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.040
After long production delays due to&nbsp;
COVID-19, "The Batman" is finally here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.160
The latest story told on screen featuring one&nbsp;
of DC Comics' most famous characters is helmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.280
by director Matt Reeves, previously known for his&nbsp;
work on "Dawn of the Planet of the Apes," "War for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.840
the Planet of the Apes," and "Cloverfield."&nbsp;
After Ben Affleck decided to step away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.800
playing the Caped Crusader, Reeves stepped&nbsp;
up to tell a new Batman story with Pattinson.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.760
"The Batman" also features a plot with the main&nbsp;
antagonist being The Riddler played by Paul Dano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.640
a character we haven't seen in live-action since&nbsp;
Cory Michael Smith played the role on "Gotham."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.000
Before that, there was 1995's "Batman Forever"&nbsp;
with Jim Carrey playing a highly charismatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.760
obsessive, and very campy version&nbsp;
of the classic Batman villain.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.200
Dano's version is incredibly dark, violent,&nbsp;
and vengeful. Although both "Batman Forever"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.560
and "The Batman" feature The Riddler&nbsp;
as one of their main antagonists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.040
there are pretty vast and obvious differences&nbsp;
between both films. For one, there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.720
massively important character missing from&nbsp;
"The Batman" that was featured in 1995 — Robin.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.040
Although nothing is really ever hinted about&nbsp;
Dick Grayson or Robin during "The Batman,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.320
an actor who had previously played&nbsp;
one of the Robins in live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.640
appeared very early on in the movie, and&nbsp;
you might've missed him in his big scene.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.280
While the opening to "The Batman" starts off&nbsp;
by introducing us to a very demented and creepy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.680
Riddler, it isn't long before moviegoers are&nbsp;
hit with a narration from Robert Pattinson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.600
Bruce Wayne. It's Halloween night and Gotham City&nbsp;
is celebrating, but as Bruce notes, crime still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.880
runs rampant. Gotham City Police Department&nbsp;
Lt. James Gordon, played by Jeffrey Wright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.520
lights up the Bat-Signal, and we see common&nbsp;
criminals on the streets start to get scared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.760
feeling fearful that somewhere nearby,&nbsp;
Batman could be hiding in the shadows.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.280
The film cuts to the subway where&nbsp;
a few face-painted men are huddling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.200
around watching a video they took of&nbsp;
themselves assaulting someone for fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.480
although the youngest in the group&nbsp;
seems to be uncomfortable with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.720
Without much dialogue, the youngest is told by&nbsp;
the leader of the group to pursue and attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.520
another person on the subway with them, and&nbsp;
as they do, they're confronted by Batman.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.880
As seen in the first trailer for&nbsp;
the film, Batman beats the leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.960
and utters an iconic line that plays an&nbsp;
important role throughout the actual film:

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.280
"I'm vengeance."

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.160
The rest of the group — except for the young kid&nbsp;
— proceed to attack Batman and fail. He and Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.120
share a look, where the kid seems simultaneously&nbsp;
relieved that Batman showed up and also amazed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.800
by what he sees — and then he runs off. This&nbsp;
kid happens to be played by actor Jay Lycurgo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.200
In the credits, the kid is referred to&nbsp;
simply as "Train Gang Young Member" — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.360
he actually plays a character who's known&nbsp;
as DC Comics' Robin in another project.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.360
If you're a DC fan, you probably saw Jay Lycurgo&nbsp;
playing Tim Drake in Season 3 of "Titans" before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.640
his quick part in "The Batman." The HBO Max show&nbsp;
actually centers around being Robin in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.800
as the first season introduces fans&nbsp;
to the original Robin, Dick Grayson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.440
who's played on the show by Brenton Thwaites.&nbsp;
The series covers his journey to step out from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.480
being under the shadow cast by Batman and&nbsp;
become his own hero — Nightwing. Soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.080
we are introduced to Curran Walters' Jason Todd,&nbsp;
and in Season 3 we are introduced to Tim Drake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.480
an intelligent young man who figures out&nbsp;
on his own that Nightwing is Dick Grayson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.640
Robin was (prior to his death) Jason&nbsp;
Todd, and that Bruce Wayne is Batman.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.840
During Season 3, Lycurgo's Tim goes through&nbsp;
quite the transformative experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.040
as he is at one point shot by Scarecrow,&nbsp;
played by Vincent Kartheiser, and nearly dies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.400
During the finale of Season 3, we see that Tim&nbsp;
is accepted by the Titans and leaves with Dick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.240
and most of the team to San Francisco and the&nbsp;
Titans Tower once more. Lycurgo is set to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.600
as a recurring character in Season 4 of the show,&nbsp;
which has already started production. It's likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.320
Tim's training from Dick or Bruce might be&nbsp;
a focus in the show's plot moving forward.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.280
It's impossible to know whether we will see&nbsp;
Lycurgo again in possible sequels to "The Batman."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.960
It seems unlikely that the actor would&nbsp;
play two different Robins in two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.280
live-action universes. Then again, we do&nbsp;
have three different actors set to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.160
three different big-screen versions of Batman&nbsp;
in the same year, so anything's possible.

